.TH ARCH 3 x86
.SH NAME
arch \- x86 architecture-specific information and control
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #P /dev

.B /dev/cputype
.B /dev/ioalloc
.B /dev/iob
.B /dev/iol
.B /dev/iow
.B /dev/irqalloc
.SH DESCRIPTION
This device presents textual information about PC hardware and allows
user-level control of the I/O ports on x86-class machines.
.PP
Reads from
.I cputype
recover the processor type and clock rate.
.PP
Reads from
.I ioalloc
return I/O ranges used by each device, one line
per range.  Each line contains three fields separated by white space: first address
in hexadecimal,
last address, name of device.
.PP
Reads from
.I irqalloc
return the enabled interrupts, one line per
interrupt.  Each line contains three fields separated by white space:
the trap number, the IRQ it is assigned to, and the name of
the device using it.
.PP
Reads and writes to
.IR iob ,
.IR iow ,
and
.I iol
cause 8-bit wide, 16-bit wide, and 32-bit wide requests to
I/O ports.
The port accessed is determined by the byte offset of the
file descriptor.
.SS "What a board adds"
The files above are the PC's.
.B #P
is not only the PC's, though: a board registers its own with
.CW addarchfile ()
and they appear here beside them, so what
.B #P
holds depends on which kernel is running.  A port's worth of hardware
that is one board's and not portable belongs here rather than in a device
of its own.
.PP
.I Os/inferno/stm32f407
adds five, and they are listed because nothing else lists them:
.TF calinfoxx
.TP
.B uid
The microcontroller's 96-bit factory serial number, read-only in silicon
(RM0090 39.1), as twenty-four hex digits.
.B "A board needs a name before anything measured about it can belong to it,"
and every name a board could be told is circular \- the thing that would
tell it is the thing that needs to know.  It is the key of
.B /lib/radio
and of
.B cal
below; see
.IR radiocal (1).
.TP
.B cal
An append-only log in the flash below the bootloader, where a board
writes down what it has measured about itself.  Read it for what is
stored and write a whole record to append one; there is no erase,
because the region is virgin flash and programming only clears bits, so
appending needs none and nothing else can be offered.  A reflash erases
it.
.TP
.B calinfo
Where that store is, how big it is and how much of it is used.
.TP
.B led
The board's two lamps, written as
.RB ` "1 on" '
or
.RB ` "2 off" '.
Reading gives the port's own output register rather than what was last
written, which is the difference between a lamp and a variable.
.TP
.B adc
The two mikroBUS analogue pins and the part's own temperature, reference
and battery channels.
.TP
.B spi2
What the SPI bus says right now: the status byte, the part number and
version, and the MISO pin read directly.  It exists because when
.CW cc1120init ()
finds no part the only account of why is a line printed at boot, which on
a board has usually scrolled away by the time anybody wants it.
.SH EXAMPLE
The following Limbo code reads from an x86 byte I/O port.
.IP
.EX
inportb(port: int): byte
{
    data := array[1] of byte;

    if(iobfd == nil)
        iobfd = sys->open("#P/iob", Sys->ORDWR);

    sys->seek(iobfd, port, 0);
    if(sys->read(iobfd, data, len data) != len data)
        fatal(sys->sprint("inportb(16r%4.4x): %r\en", port));
    return data[0];
}
.EE
